UTMP(5)                        P32W Unix                         UTMP(5)

NAME
       utmp - login records

SYNOPSIS
       #include <utmp.h>

       struct utmp {
           char ut_line[8];    /* tty0 */
           char ut_name[8];    /* empty: nobody */
           int ut_time;
       };
       #define UTMP "/etc/utmp"

DESCRIPTION
       /etc/utmp records who is logged in on each terminal. It
       holds one 20-byte struct utmp per terminal, indexed by the
       terminal's number: the record for ttyN is at byte offset
       20 * N. ut_line is the terminal's name, ut_name the login
       name, and ut_time the time of the login in seconds since
       1970 (UTC). Neither string need end in a NUL when it is 8
       characters long. A record whose ut_name is empty is not in
       use.

       login(1) writes the record when a user logs in; init(8)
       clears it (all zeros) when that terminal's session ends.
       who(1), uptime(1) and top(1) read the file. The file as
       shipped is empty; it grows as the terminals are used.

FILES
       /etc/utmp

SEE ALSO
       who(1), login(1), init(8), uptime(1), top(1)

BUGS
       There is no wtmp: logins are not logged anywhere. su(1)
       does not change the record. Nothing clears the file at
       boot: after a crash a terminal's old record stays until the
       next login there, so who may list users who are gone.
